          "source_quote": "καὶ δὴ μέχρι ποῦ· μηκύνω τὸν λόγον, μὴ βουλόμενος σημῆναι τὸ λυποῦν, ὃ ἐξ ἀνάγκης κἂν βραδὺ πάντως εἰπεῖν με δεῖ;",
          "english_quote": "and indeed, how long shall I prolong the account, unwilling to disclose the painful thing that necessity compels me, even if late, in any event to tell? This same man",
          "issue": "The English punctuation leaves the opening construction, ‘Let it be known to you ... that Simon’, without a completed predicate. The Greek brackets the rhetorical interruption and then resumes Simon with οὗτος αὐτός, leading ultimately to his violent death. The draft instead starts a separate sentence whose principal assertion is his proclamation. The claims are present, but their governing syntactic relationship is obscured. The printed high point is an editorial feature, not evidence of source corruption.",
          "suggested_revision": "Bracket the entire rhetorical interruption with paired dashes or parentheses, then resume and complete the original ‘that Simon’ construction. Keep the proclamation subordinate, for example with ‘having ... proclaimed’, and carry the sentence through his departure from life. Retain the disclosed uncertainty about the rhetorical question’s scope. The revised sentence requires fresh review.",
