          "source_quote": "καὶ σὲ δὲ περὶ ὧν χρὴ ἀμέριμνον εἶναι οἱ πάντες ποιεῖν ἐὰν μὴ νοῶσιν, παρὰ τῶν διακόνων μανθανέτωσαν",
          "english_quote": "Moreover, if they all do not understand that they must keep you free from concern about the matters from which you must be free, let them learn it from the deacons",
          "issue": "‘The matters from which you must be free’ changes freedom from concern about those matters into freedom from the matters themselves. The Greek has περὶ ὧν with ἀμέριμνον εἶναι: being without care concerning them. The draft also expresses two separate ‘must’ relationships where the marked Greek construction contains one χρὴ. Its attachment to εἶναι and its relationship to ποιεῖν deserve disclosure rather than silent expansion. This is a syntactic and interpretive question, not evidence of an OCR error or missing text.",
          "suggested_revision": "Recast the sentence so that the freedom is explicitly freedom from concern about the matters. Compare the provisional candidate ‘Moreover, if they all do not understand about which matters they ought to keep you free from concern, let them learn from the deacons.’ In source_issues, disclose whether χρὴ is understood as governing their making you free from concern or your being free from concern; do not posit a Greek correction merely because the word order is marked. Review the revised wording afresh.",
